---
// The key to an expanded board row: what each event id is, why one case carries
// several, and how the probability and the call relate. Static copy over the
// moment register in src/lib/moments.js. Collapsed by default so the board stays
// near the top of the page.
import { MOMENTS, STAGES, STAGE_ORDER } from "../lib/moments.js";

const groups = STAGE_ORDER.map((stage) => ({
  stage,
  ...STAGES[stage],
  events: Object.entries(MOMENTS).filter(([, v]) => v.stage === stage),
}));
---
<details class="key" id="reading-a-case">
  <summary>How to read an expanded case</summary>

  <h2>Events</h2>
  <p>
    An event is one question about one case, asked at one point in time. The id says which. Three stages, each with its own question:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Event</th><th>Forecast taken</th></tr>
      </thead>
      {groups.map((g) => (
        <tbody>
          <tr class="stage"><th colspan="2">{g.label} — {g.question}</th></tr>
          {g.events.map(([id, v]) => (
            <tr><td class="mono">{id}</td><td>{v.when}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      ))}
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Why one case has several events</h2>
  <p>
    The question stays the same within a stage; what is known does not. A petition read at docketing, the same petition after a relist,
    and the same petition after the Solicitor General weighs in are three different forecasting problems. Each gets its own event,
    freezes its own snapshot of the docket, and is scored on its own against the same eventual outcome, so an early forecast is never
    overwritten by a later, better-informed one. A case that is granted then opens a merits event, which asks a new question.
    Most cases carry one event; only the ones that reach a further moment carry more.
  </p>

  <h2>The probability and the call</h2>
  <p>
    Every prediction states one belief twice. The <strong>probability</strong> is the scored number: P(granted) at cert and interim,
    P(disturbed) at merits, meaning the judgment below is reversed or vacated, in whole or in part. It is graded by Brier score once the event resolves.
    The <strong>call</strong> is the single most likely outcome label, from a wider vocabulary
    (<span class="mono">granted</span>, <span class="mono">denied</span>, <span class="mono">granted-in-part</span>, <span class="mono">gvr</span>,
    <span class="mono">summary-reversal</span>, <span class="mono">dismissed</span>, <span class="mono">withdrawn</span>), and is checked as a plain hit or miss.
  </p>
  <p>
    So “P(granted) 12%, called <em>denied</em>” is one position, not two: a denial is most likely, and the model puts a grant at about one in eight.
    Nearly every petition is denied, so nearly every call reads <em>denied</em>; the probability is where the models actually differ.
    Two wrinkles. At cert, <span class="mono">gvr</span>, <span class="mono">summary-reversal</span> and <span class="mono">granted-in-part</span> all count as grants,
    so such a call travels with a high probability. At interim, the probability is for an unqualified grant: an order granting relief in part resolves as denied.
    Merits events carry no call; the forecast there is the judgment and a vote for each Justice, in the linked prediction.
  </p>
</details>

<style>
  .key { margin: 1.2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .key > summary { cursor: pointer; padding: 0.6rem 0; font-weight: 560; }
  .key[open] { padding-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .key h2 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 1.4em 0 0.4em; }
  .key h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 0.6em; }
  .key p { max-width: var(--measure); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .key table { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .key .table-scroll table { min-width: 0; }
  .key tr.stage th { font-weight: 560; font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--muted); border-bottom-color: var(--rule); padding-top: 0.9rem; }
  .key td.mono { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; padding-right: 1rem; }
</style>
